WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Propyl ethanoate is an ester that smells a

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.160
lot like pears.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.880
Suppose the only carbon compounds you're allowed to

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.520
start from are ethene, a gas, and 1-bromopropane,

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.560
a haloalkane.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.280
Neither one looks anything like the ester.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.560
Making a target compound from simpler ones is

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.840
called a synthesis.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.680
So how do you plan a route from

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.080
what you have to what you want, and

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.520
show it as a flow chart?

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:30.813
Good news: you've already met every reaction you

00:00:30.894 --> 00:00:31.453
need.

00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:33.253
Put them all on one map.

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:36.093
Each box is a family of compounds, and

00:00:36.174 --> 00:00:38.333
each arrow is a reaction from an earlier

00:00:38.414 --> 00:00:39.373
video.

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:42.013
You've seen the middle already: the alcohol is

00:00:42.094 --> 00:00:43.053
the hub.

00:00:43.174 --> 00:00:47.133
Alkenes and haloalkanes make alcohols, and alcohols lead

00:00:47.214 --> 00:00:52.333
on to alkenes, haloalkanes, aldehydes, ketones and acids.

00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:54.413
Now add the joining arrows.

00:00:54.494 --> 00:00:58.013
Acids join alcohols to make esters, or amines

00:00:58.054 --> 00:00:59.453
to make amides.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.773
And alkanes need a halogen and U V

00:01:01.854 --> 00:01:03.853
light before they can join in at all.

00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:08.346
Move one: name the functional group in the

00:01:08.427 --> 00:01:11.226
target and in each starting material, and count

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:12.346
the carbons.

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.906
The reactions on this map change the functional

00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:17.706
group, but they don't break the carbon chain,

00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:19.466
so the chain keeps its length.

00:01:20.107 --> 00:01:22.506
The only arrows that join two molecules are

00:01:22.587 --> 00:01:25.146
the ones that make esters and amides, so

00:01:25.227 --> 00:01:26.506
their carbons add up.

00:01:26.987 --> 00:01:29.786
Propyl ethanoate has an ethanoate part, with two

00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:32.506
carbons, and a propyl part, with three.

00:01:33.147 --> 00:01:37.146
Ethene has two carbons, and 1-bromopropane has three.

00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:39.226
The counts already match.

00:01:39.627 --> 00:01:41.946
If they didn't, you'd need a joining step,

00:01:42.027 --> 00:01:43.226
or a different start.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:48.033
Move two: work backwards from the target.

00:01:48.514 --> 00:01:51.073
Ask which reaction makes its functional group.

00:01:51.714 --> 00:01:54.193
An ester comes from a carboxylic acid and

00:01:54.234 --> 00:01:56.913
an alcohol, so cut it at the single-bonded

00:01:57.074 --> 00:01:58.913
oxygen, like in the esters video.

00:01:59.554 --> 00:02:02.433
The ethanoate part needs ethanoic acid, and the

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:05.233
propyl part needs propan-1-ol.

00:02:05.314 --> 00:02:07.313
Now ask the same question about each of

00:02:07.394 --> 00:02:08.353
those.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:11.873
Ethanoic acid comes from oxidising a primary alcohol,

00:02:11.954 --> 00:02:12.833
ethanol.

00:02:12.914 --> 00:02:15.713
And ethanol comes from ethene and steam, one

00:02:15.794 --> 00:02:17.473
of our starting materials.

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:19.953
Keep asking until you reach something you're allowed

00:02:20.034 --> 00:02:20.913
to start from.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.093
Where does propan-1-ol come from?

00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:27.653
Here, from 1-bromopropane.

00:02:28.134 --> 00:02:30.853
Hydroxide takes the bromine's place on the same

00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:33.733
carbon, so the O H lands exactly where

00:02:33.814 --> 00:02:35.573
you need it, on carbon one.

00:02:36.214 --> 00:02:38.453
Why not use an alkene instead?

00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:40.293
Suppose you had propene.

00:02:40.414 --> 00:02:42.773
Its double bond isn't symmetrical, so the O

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:45.013
H can go on either carbon, and most

00:02:45.094 --> 00:02:46.773
of it ends up on the middle one.

00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:49.253
You'd get mainly the wrong alcohol.

00:02:49.414 --> 00:02:52.293
Adding H Br to propene first doesn't help,

00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:54.293
because the bromine mostly lands on the middle

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.653
carbon too.

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:58.133
So when the position matters, start from a

00:02:58.214 --> 00:03:00.773
haloalkane that already has its halogen on the

00:03:00.854 --> 00:03:01.813
right carbon.

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:06.833
Move four: write the route forwards, in the

00:03:06.874 --> 00:03:08.633
order you'd carry it out, and put the

00:03:08.714 --> 00:03:10.953
conditions on every arrow.

00:03:11.074 --> 00:03:14.233
Ethene and steam, with a phosphoric acid catalyst,

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:16.953
about three hundred degrees and high pressure, give

00:03:17.034 --> 00:03:18.153
ethanol.

00:03:18.274 --> 00:03:21.113
Ethanol here is an intermediate, a compound made

00:03:21.194 --> 00:03:22.753
on the way and used up in the

00:03:22.794 --> 00:03:23.993
next step.

00:03:24.154 --> 00:03:28.073
Then acidified dichromate in excess, heated under reflux,

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:30.393
takes the ethanol all the way to ethanoic

00:03:30.474 --> 00:03:31.273
acid.

00:03:31.354 --> 00:03:34.793
Reflux, not distillation, so the aldehyde can't escape

00:03:34.874 --> 00:03:36.793
before it's oxidised again.

00:03:36.874 --> 00:03:40.313
On the other arm, 1-bromopropane heated under reflux

00:03:40.434 --> 00:03:44.073
with aqueous sodium hydroxide gives propan-1-ol.

00:03:44.394 --> 00:03:46.793
Finally, the acid and the alcohol, with a

00:03:46.874 --> 00:03:49.833
few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid, heated under

00:03:49.954 --> 00:03:52.473
reflux, make the ester and water.

00:03:52.714 --> 00:03:55.033
That's an equilibrium, like the hydration at the

00:03:55.114 --> 00:03:58.153
start, so both arrows get the double arrow.

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:03.846
Then finish move four: check every arrow, because

00:04:04.007 --> 00:04:06.526
one wrong arrow breaks the whole route.

00:04:06.887 --> 00:04:09.126
Here's a plan for propanoic acid that fails

00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:10.006
twice.

00:04:10.247 --> 00:04:13.286
It turns propane straight into propan-2-ol.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:15.726
No reaction we've met does that.

00:04:16.007 --> 00:04:18.406
An alkane first needs a halogen and U

00:04:18.487 --> 00:04:20.406
V light, and that gives a mixture.

00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:25.046
Then it oxidises propan-2-ol to propanone, and propanone

00:04:25.207 --> 00:04:26.406
to propanoic acid.

00:04:26.807 --> 00:04:28.446
That arrow doesn't exist.

00:04:28.727 --> 00:04:31.366
A ketone has no hydrogen on its carbonyl

00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:34.886
carbon, so acidified dichromate can't oxidise it any

00:04:34.967 --> 00:04:35.446
further.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.766
It's a dead end: no arrow on the

00:04:37.847 --> 00:04:38.646
map leaves it.

00:04:39.207 --> 00:04:41.126
To reach the acid, you need the primary

00:04:41.207 --> 00:04:43.126
alcohol, propan-1-ol.

00:04:43.607 --> 00:04:45.526
And keep routes short where you can.

00:04:45.927 --> 00:04:49.126
Every step loses some product, so extra steps

00:04:49.207 --> 00:04:52.166
usually mean a lower overall yield, the amount

00:04:52.207 --> 00:04:53.686
of product left at the end.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.700
So, name and count, work backwards, choose, then

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.220
write forwards and check.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.660
In short, work backwards to plan, because the

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.140
target's group tells you the last step, and

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.100
write forwards to answer.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.300
Many routes run along the main road from

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:16.020
ethene: alkene, primary alcohol, acid, ester.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.740
When the O H's position matters, start from

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.220
a haloalkane with its halogen on the right

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:21.860
carbon.

00:05:23.867 --> 00:05:24.506
Your turn.

00:05:24.987 --> 00:05:27.706
A chemist wants to make pentanoic acid, and

00:05:27.787 --> 00:05:32.026
can start from either pent-1-ene or 1-chloropentane.

00:05:32.347 --> 00:05:34.986
Construct a flow chart for a two-step synthesis

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:37.786
from the better starting material, with the reagents

00:05:37.867 --> 00:05:40.506
and conditions for each step, and justify your

00:05:40.587 --> 00:05:41.546
choice.

00:05:41.707 --> 00:05:42.906
Pause here to answer.

00:05:54.134 --> 00:05:56.053
Start from 1-chloropentane.

00:05:56.534 --> 00:06:00.373
Aqueous sodium hydroxide, heated under reflux, puts the

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:03.253
O H on the same carbon, carbon one,

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:05.173
giving pentan-1-ol.

00:06:05.494 --> 00:06:10.053
Then excess acidified dichromate under reflux oxidises that

00:06:10.134 --> 00:06:12.373
primary alcohol to pentanoic acid.

00:06:13.094 --> 00:06:15.573
Starting from pent-1-ene is the trap.

00:06:16.054 --> 00:06:17.653
Most of the water adds its O H

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:21.733
to carbon two, giving mainly pentan-2-ol, and that

00:06:21.814 --> 00:06:23.733
only oxidises to a ketone.

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:26.373
Last one.

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:29.973
A chemist has only ethene and butan-1-ol to

00:06:30.054 --> 00:06:30.693
start from.

00:06:31.254 --> 00:06:34.053
Draw a flow chart to make ethyl butanoate,

00:06:34.134 --> 00:06:36.933
with the reagents and conditions on every arrow.

00:06:37.414 --> 00:06:38.773
Pause here to answer.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.560
The ethyl part comes from ethanol, and the

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:54.960
butanoate part from butanoic acid.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.320
So hydrate ethene with steam and a phosphoric

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.880
acid catalyst, hot and under pressure.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:05.600
Oxidise the butan-1-ol with excess acidified dichromate, under

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:06.640
reflux.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.720
Then esterify with concentrated sulfuric acid under reflux,

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:12.240
using a double arrow.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.360
Oxidising the ethanol instead is the trap.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.160
That makes butyl ethanoate, an isomer.

00:07:20.167 --> 00:07:23.046
So every synthesis question uses the same four

00:07:23.207 --> 00:07:25.006
moves, on one map.

00:07:25.287 --> 00:07:28.246
Every arrow needs its reagents and conditions, and

00:07:28.407 --> 00:07:29.846
every box needs a name.

00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:34.486
Next: practice, with harder targets and starting materials

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:35.526
to plan from.

00:07:37.534 --> 00:07:38.973
Here are the key notes.

00:07:39.214 --> 00:07:41.133
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:41.214 --> 00:07:42.013
to copy them.
